    47	  old_logic_vs_new_logic:
    48	    - original main() assumed one square side n and one global feasible-window size
    49	    - new main() must detect a solid rectangle, keep its orientation fixed, and test
    50	      h x w windows instead of n x n windows
    51	    - original generator only needed one chokepoint; new generator must ensure several
    52	      visible gate levels exist in one corridor and that only a later gate blocks the
    53	      mover in stuck cases
    54	  why_this_is_a_true_fusion:
    55	    - D4 changes the object from square to rectangle, which makes horizontal and
    56	      vertical clearance requirements different
    57	    - D2 adds multiple gate levels instead of one bottleneck
    58	    - D9 makes the whole scene non-square so the long horizontal and vertical runs
    59	      are visually asymmetric and not reducible to the original square-grid task
    60	
    61	b_complexity_calibration:
    62	  - Only one mover/target pair is used, so there is no ambiguous matching problem.
    63	  - Rectangle shapes come from a small repeated family:
    64	    (2,3), (3,2), (2,4), (4,2), (3,4), (4,3)
    65	  - The corridor always has exactly five stages and four turns. That is enough to
    66	    show multi-level gating without producing visually chaotic mazes.
    67	  - Stage widths are sampled from only three passable levels per axis:
    68	    horizontal heights in {h, h+1, h+2}
    69	    vertical widths in {w, w+1, w+2}
    70	    plus one optional failing level in {1..h-1} or {1..w-1}.
    71	    This keeps the visual rule discrete and observable.
    72	
    73	c_implicit_assumption_audit_of_original_main:
    74	  - "bbox_h == bbox_w" is no longer valid; solid non-square rectangles must be kept.
    75	  - "choose the most common side length n" is no longer valid; the relevant key is the
    76	    exact ordered shape (h, w), and mover/dest are identified by their visible colors.
    77	  - "grid is square" is no longer valid; all loops must use the actual H and W.
    78	  - "one scalar width n governs all segments" is no longer valid; feasibility still
    79	    comes from whole-window BFS, but the generator must encode anisotropic gate widths
    80	    so that horizontal and vertical failures happen for different reasons.
    81	  - "one chokepoint is enough" is no longer valid; the solver still runs one BFS, but
    82	    the generator must guarantee several visible gate levels so the fused behavior is
    83	    inferable from examples.
    84	
    85	d_placement_legality:
    86	  - The snake path is specified by six top-left anchor positions with gaps larger than
    87	    the mover size, so the turn boxes at adjacent anchors do not accidentally merge
    88	    across an entire blocked stage.
    89	  - Every painted corridor rectangle is clipped by construction to stay in-bounds.
    90	  - The mover start and target footprints are explicitly painted as full h x w blocks
    91	    before recoloring them black/red, so both tokens are always solid legal objects.
    92	  - The generator oracle-checks every sample by recomputing reachability and verifying
    93	    main(input) equals the intended output.
    94	
    95	e_visual_observability:
    96	  - The mover is visibly rectangular in every sample because h != w always holds.
    97	  - The river itself exposes the rule: horizontal and vertical stages have visibly
    98	    different thicknesses, and those thicknesses vary by stage.
    99	  - In stuck samples, the mover is not blocked immediately. It passes earlier wider
   100	    gates and only stops deeper in the snake, which makes the "multi-level" aspect
   101	    directly visible from input/output pairs.
   102	  - No hidden sorting by raw color ids is used beyond the semantic role colors that
   103	    are directly visible to a human: black = mover, red = destination.
   104	
   105	f_visual_cleanliness:
   106	  - Only the original four colors are kept.
   107	  - The scene is one connected river with one mover and one target; there are no extra
   108	    markers, labels, or decorative objects.
   109	  - The five-stage snake is structured and sparse enough that each gate can be seen
   110	    without crowding.
   111	
   112	g_new_main_assumptions_and_generator_guarantees:
   113	  - Assumption: exactly one black solid rectangle and one red solid rectangle share the
   114	    same non-square shape.
   115	    Guarantee: generator places exactly one such pair.
   116	  - Assumption: the river is the most common non-background color besides black/red.
   117	    Guarantee: generator uses only one river color and paints most non-background area
   118	    with it.
   119	  - Assumption: the black start position is itself a feasible h x w placement.
   120	    Guarantee: the first horizontal stage is always passable.
   121	  - Assumption: non-square behavior matters.
   122	    Guarantee: generator only uses h != w and H != W.
   123	"""
   124	
   125	
   126	_BG, _RIVER, _MOVER, _DEST = 3, 1, 0, 2
   127	_RECT_SHAPES = [(2, 3), (3, 2), (2, 4), (4, 2), (3, 4), (4, 3)]
   128	_DIR4 = [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]
   129	
   130	
   131	def _background_color(grid: np.ndarray) -> int:
   132	    corners = [int(grid[0, 0]), int(grid[0, -1]), int(grid[-1, 0]), int(grid[-1, -1])]
   133	    return int(Counter(corners).most_common(1)[0][0])
   134	
   135	
   136	def _solid_rectangles(grid: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> list[dict]:
   137	    rects: list[dict] = []
   138	    for comp in find_components(grid, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, return_format="dict"):
   139	        color = int(comp["color"])
   140	        r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, comp["bbox"])
   141	        h = int(bbox_h(comp["bbox"]))
   142	        w = int(bbox_w(comp["bbox"]))
   143	        patch = grid[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]
   144	        if bool((patch == color).all()):
   145	            rects.append({"color": color, "r0": r0, "c0": c0, "h": h, "w": w})
   146	    return rects
   147	
   148	
   149	def _choose_token_pair(rects: list[dict]) -> tuple[dict, dict]:
   150	    movers = [rect for rect in rects if int(rect["color"]) == _MOVER and int(rect["h"]) != int(rect["w"])]
   151	    dests = [rect for rect in rects if int(rect["color"]) == _DEST and int(rect["h"]) != int(rect["w"])]
   152	    assert movers and dests
   153	
   154	    shared_shapes = sorted(
   155	        set((int(rect["h"]), int(rect["w"])) for rect in movers)
   156	        & set((int(rect["h"]), int(rect["w"])) for rect in dests)
   157	    )
   158	    assert shared_shapes
   159	
   160	    shape = max(shared_shapes, key=lambda hw: (hw[0] * hw[1], hw[0] + hw[1], hw[0]))
   161	    mover = next(rect for rect in movers if (int(rect["h"]), int(rect["w"])) == shape)
   162	    dest = next(rect for rect in dests if (int(rect["h"]), int(rect["w"])) == shape)
   163	    return mover, dest
   164	
   165	
   166	def _river_color(grid: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> int:
   167	    counts = Counter(map(int, grid.flatten()))
   168	    for color, _count in counts.most_common():
   169	        if int(color) not in (int(bg), _MOVER, _DEST):
   170	            return int(color)
   171	    raise AssertionError("river color not found")
   172	
   173	
   174	def _feasible_placements(trav: np.ndarray, h: int, w: int) -> np.ndarray:
   175	    H, W = trav.shape
   176	    h2, w2 = H - h + 1, W - w + 1
   177	    assert h2 > 0 and w2 > 0
   178	    feasible = np.zeros((h2, w2), dtype=bool)
   179	    for r in range(h2):
   180	        for c in range(w2):
   181	            feasible[r, c] = bool(trav[r : r + h, c : c + w].all())
   182	    return feasible
   183	
   184	
   185	def _bfs_placement(feasible: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int) -> np.ndarray:
   186	    h2, w2 = feasible.shape
   187	    dist = -np.ones((h2, w2), dtype=int)
   188	    dist[sr, sc] = 0
   189	    q = deque([(sr, sc)])
   190	    while q:
   191	        r, c = q.popleft()
   192	        for dr, dc in _DIR4:
   193	            rr, cc = r + dr, c + dc
   194	            if 0 <= rr < h2 and 0 <= cc < w2 and feasible[rr, cc] and dist[rr, cc] == -1:
   195	                dist[rr, cc] = dist[r, c] + 1
   196	                q.append((rr, cc))
   197	    return dist
   198	
   199	
   200	def _farthest_reachable(dist: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   201	    best = -1
   202	    tr, tc = sr, sc
   203	    for r in range(dist.shape[0]):
   204	        for c in range(dist.shape[1]):
   205	            if int(dist[r, c]) > best:
   206	                best = int(dist[r, c])
   207	                tr, tc = r, c
   208	    return tr, tc
   209	
   210	
   211	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   212	    """
   213	    Slide a solid rectangle through a multi-gate winding corridor on a non-square grid.
   214	
   215	    The rectangle never rotates. Horizontal stages need height >= h; vertical stages
   216	    need width >= w. If all gates are passable, the mover covers the red rectangle.
   217	    Otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable placement before the first failing gate.
   218	    """
   219	    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)
   220	    H, W = g.shape
   221	
   222	    bg = _background_color(g)
   223	    mover, dest = _choose_token_pair(_solid_rectangles(g, bg))
   224	    river = _river_color(g, bg)
   225	
   226	    h = int(mover["h"])
   227	    w = int(mover["w"])
   228	    sr, sc = int(mover["r0"]), int(mover["c0"])
   229	    dr, dc = int(dest["r0"]), int(dest["c0"])
   230	
   231	    trav = (g == river) | (g == _MOVER) | (g == _DEST)
   232	    h2, w2 = H - h + 1, W - w + 1
   233	    assert h2 > 0 and w2 > 0
   234	    assert 0 <= sr < h2 and 0 <= sc < w2
   235	
   236	    feasible = _feasible_placements(trav, h, w)
   237	    assert feasible[sr, sc]
   238	    dist = _bfs_placement(feasible, sr, sc)
   239	
   240	    if 0 <= dr < h2 and 0 <= dc < w2 and feasible[dr, dc] and dist[dr, dc] >= 0:
   241	        tr, tc = dr, dc
   242	    else:
   243	        tr, tc = _farthest_reachable(dist, sr, sc)
   244	
   245	    out = g.copy()
   246	    out[sr : sr + h, sc : sc + w] = river
   247	    out[tr : tr + h, tc : tc + w] = _MOVER
   248	    return out
   249	
   250	
   251	def _paint_h(grid: np.ndarray, r: int, c0: int, c1: int, band_h: int, mover_w: int) -> None:
   252	    left, right = sorted((c0, c1))
   253	    grid[r : r + band_h, left : right + mover_w] = _RIVER
   254	
   255	
   256	def _paint_v(grid: np.ndarray, r0: int, r1: int, c: int, band_w: int, mover_h: int) -> None:
   257	    top, bottom = sorted((r0, r1))
   258	    grid[top : bottom + mover_h, c : c + band_w] = _RIVER
   259	
